.TH "WINDOW.GETSELECTION" "3JS" "October 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Window.getSelection \- Window: getSelection() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getSelection()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Window\f[R]
interface returns the \f[CR]Selection\f[R] object associated with the
window\(cqs \f[CR]document\f[R], representing the range of text selected
by the user, or the current position of the caret.
.PP
\f[CR]window.getSelection()\f[R] is a shorthand for
\f[CR]window.document.getSelection()\f[R].
Read \f[CR]Document.getSelection()\f[R] for more information.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getSelection()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Selection\f[R] object, or \f[CR]null\f[R] if the associated
document has no browsing context (for example, the window is an
\f[CR]<iframe>\f[R] that is not attached to a document).
.PP
When called on an \f[CR]<iframe>\f[R] that is not displayed (e.g., where
\f[CR]display: none\f[R] is set) Firefox returns \f[CR]null\f[R],
whereas other browsers returns a \f[CR]Selection\f[R] object with
\f[CR]Selection.type\f[R] set to \f[CR]None\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Getting a Selection object
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] selection = window.getSelection();
\f[B]const\f[R] selRange = selection.getRangeAt(0);
\f[I]// do stuff with the range\f[R]

console.log(selection); \f[I]// Selection object\f[R]
.EE
